ENGLISH 88

&
\begin{verbatim}
%
%SUBGRAMMAR PreppformulaTOprop

    (* TC_PPPPROstatus *)
( TpppNoPROsubj/1        | TpppPROsubj/2 )
.
    (* RC_PPPSubstitution *)
{ RpppNPsubst/3          | RpppPREPPsubst/4     | 
  RpppADVPsubst/5        | RpppSubjSubst/8     }
.
TpppAspectNeutralization/9
.
    (* RC_PPPMood *)
( ROpenPPPMood/6            | RClosedPPPMood/7 )
.
    (* RC_pppPunc *)
RpppNoPunc/9

HEAD:
<
PREPPFORMULA     FROM (PREPPPROPtoPREPPFORMULA)
>

(* IMPORT: *)
<
 3: < ARGUMENT 2    NP     FROM (NPformation)     >
 4: < ARGUMENT 2    PREPP  FROM (PreppFormation)  >
 5: < ARGUMENT 2    ADVP   FROM (AdvpFormation)   >
 8: < ARGUMENT 2    NP     FROM (NPformation)     >
>

EXPORT:
<
OPENPREPPPROP
CLOSEDPREPPPROP
>

&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage

\begin{verbatim}
% TRANSFORMATION TpppNoPROsubj
< m1: I1::PREPPFORMULA{PREPPFORMULArec1}[mu1]
>
  
< m : I1::PREPPFORMULA{PREPPFORMULArec1}[mu1]
>
  
   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: PREPPFORMULArec1.PROsubject = false
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >

&
\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TpppNoPROsubj
\item[* relevant examples:]
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] preppformulaTOprop
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) TC\_pppPROstatus
\item[* date of creation:] 19-AUG-1988
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] Dutch FpppPREprosubject
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] to make PPPs without a PROsubject (closed PPPs). In generation, 
all incoming PPFs have a free choice between this transformation and the 
following one, which makes open PPPs.
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\
This rule should work only if subject substitution in the clause in which this 
PROP 
will end up is impossible, since it is the order of substitution in the main 
clause that reflects scope (see comment in RpppSubjSubst). A situation like 
that will occur in cases where 
the PROP ends up embedded in another structure, while the substitution rules 
can look only one level deep. To prevent two ambiguous substitution paths, one 
here and one in the main clause, the rules for the main clause must be 
formulated in such a way that they do not accept PROPs that have already been 
substituted into.
\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
% TRANSFORMATION TpppPROsubj
< m1: I1::PREPPFORMULA{PREPPFORMULArec1}[mu1]
>

< m : PREPPFORMULA{PREPPFORMULArec2}[mu1]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: PREPPFORMULArec1.PROsubject = false
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: PREPPFORMULArec2 := PREPPFORMULArec1;
              PREPPFORMULArec2.PROsubject := true;
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: PREPPFORMULArec2.PROsubject = true
          A1: PREPPFORMULArec1 := PREPPFORMULArec2;
              PREPPFORMULArec1.PROsubject := false;
         >

&

\end{verbatim}
